"""Focused OS and database examples where a grouped example misses a topic."""
from . import T

T('7-synchronization','critical-section-properties','Critical section','Mutual exclusion does not guarantee bounded waiting',r'''
A critical section is the part of an execution that accesses a shared invariant, such as a queue's count and links. A correct entry protocol must establish mutual exclusion: at most one execution enters at a time. Progress means contenders can make an entry decision when the critical section is free, without depending on a process that is outside the competition. Bounded waiting limits how often other contenders may enter after a process requests entry.
These are separate properties. An atomic test-and-set lock can prevent simultaneous owners while still allowing one unlucky thread to lose repeatedly. Atomicity of the entry instruction does not imply fairness of the scheduler or lock protocol. To analyze an algorithm, identify the shared entry state, show why two acquisitions cannot both succeed, and then examine whether an already waiting thread can be overtaken indefinitely. One legal starvation trace disproves bounded waiting; demonstrating a successful execution does not prove all three properties.
''',"""A spinlock starts unlocked. Its atomic test-and-set returns the previous value and sets it to locked. A and B repeatedly request it; no queue or fairness rule exists.""",r"""First simultaneous attempt::::If A observes unlocked, its atomic instruction sets locked before B can observe the old value. B therefore observes locked and waits.~Release and reacquire::::A releases, then reaches test-and-set before B on every repetition. This ordering is permitted by the stated protocol.~Separate properties::::Only A owns the lock at each instant, but B can be overtaken arbitrarily many times. Mutual exclusion holds while bounded waiting is not guaranteed.""", """The lock enforces mutual exclusion but provides no bounded-waiting guarantee.""","""No trace contains two simultaneous owners. Adding a FIFO entry queue would exclude the repeated-overtaking trace and changes the fairness guarantee.""")

T('7-scheduling','fcfs-arrivals','FCFS','FCFS dispatch with arrivals and an idle interval',r'''
First-come, first-served scheduling maintains ready processes in arrival order. In its usual nonpreemptive single-CPU model, the running process retains the CPU until its burst completes; a later arrival joins the queue rather than interrupting it. The CPU must remain idle when the ready queue is empty. Arrival timestamps, including ties, determine queue order, so an answer must state a tie rule when needed.
Construct the timeline by moving to the next arrival if nothing is ready, then dispatching the oldest ready process. Completion minus arrival gives turnaround. For a workload with one CPU burst per process and no I/O, waiting equals turnaround minus burst, and response equals first start minus arrival. These equalities rely on the model: blocked I/O time must be accounted for separately in other workloads. Always calculate each process's metrics before averaging; averaging burst lengths cannot reconstruct waiting caused by queue position or idle intervals.
''',"""P1 arrives at 0 with burst 3, P2 at 1 with burst 2, P3 at 8 with burst 1. Use nonpreemptive FCFS and zero switching cost.""",r"""Dispatch existing work::::P1 runs 0–3; P2 arrived while P1 was running and therefore runs next, 3–5.~Handle the empty queue::::No process is ready at time 5. The CPU is idle 5–8, then P3 runs 8–9.~Calculate metrics::W=(0,2,0),\quad T=(3,4,1)::Waiting is start minus arrival here; turnaround is completion minus arrival. The mean waiting time is 2/3.""", """Order P1,P2,P3 with idle time 5–8; mean waiting 2/3 time unit and mean turnaround 8/3.""","""Total CPU service is 3+2+1=6 and idle time is 3; the timeline ends at 9. P3 does not wait during time before its own arrival.""")

T('7-scheduling','sjf-ready-selection','SJF','SJF chooses the shortest burst among arrived jobs',r'''
Nonpreemptive shortest-job-first scheduling chooses the ready process with the smallest CPU burst whenever the CPU becomes available. Ready is essential: a short process arriving in the future cannot be selected now. Once a chosen process begins, later shorter arrivals do not preempt it under this version. Shortest-remaining-time-first is the preemptive alternative and can produce a different timeline.
At each dispatch time, list arrived but unfinished processes, compare their bursts and use the stated tie rule. Advance time to the selected completion, admitting any intervening arrivals into the ready set. If that set is empty, move to the next arrival. Completion minus arrival gives turnaround; subtract burst to obtain waiting for a single-burst CPU-only workload. Sorting every process globally by burst ignores arrival constraints and is a common wrong shortcut. Distinguish the dispatch rule from the idealized average-wait optimality result, whose assumptions do not give a scheduler knowledge of future arrivals.
''',"""P1=(arrival 0,burst 7), P2=(2,4), P3=(4,1), P4=(5,4). Use nonpreemptive SJF, earliest arrival for equal bursts, and no switch cost.""",r"""Time zero ready set::::Only P1 has arrived, so it runs 0–7. P3 arriving with burst 1 at time 4 cannot interrupt a nonpreemptive burst.~Choose at time seven::::Ready jobs are P2(4), P3(1), P4(4). Choose P3 for 7–8, then P2 for 8–12 because it wins the equal-burst arrival tie, then P4 for 12–16.~Compute waiting::W=(7-0-7,12-2-4,8-4-1,16-5-4)=(0,6,3,7)::Use each job's own completion, arrival and service rather than its position in a global burst sort.""", """Timeline P1 0–7, P3 7–8, P2 8–12, P4 12–16; mean waiting 4 and mean turnaround 8.""","""Waiting totals 16; bursts total 16; turnarounds (7,10,4,11) total 32. At every dispatch the selected burst is smallest among jobs already ready.""")

T('7-virtual-files','allocation-fragmentation','Memory management','Enough free memory can still fail a contiguous allocation',r'''
Memory management must choose a representation and an allocation policy before free-space arithmetic has meaning. A variable-size contiguous allocator needs one hole large enough for a requested region. External fragmentation occurs when free space is spread across separated holes: the sum may suffice while no individual hole does. Compaction can merge such space by moving allocated regions, but requires the system to support relocating them.
Paging allocates fixed-size frames, so a process can occupy nonadjacent physical frames. This removes the need for one contiguous physical run for ordinary paged allocation. It can still waste space inside the last allocated frame, which is internal fragmentation. To compare the two models, test the largest contiguous hole first, then calculate the number of whole pages needed using a ceiling and the unused bytes inside them. Do not call every unallocated byte internal fragmentation: free holes between allocated regions and unused space within an allocated unit have different causes.
''',"""Free holes are 6 KiB, 4 KiB and 4 KiB. A process requests 11 KiB. Compare contiguous allocation with paging using 2 KiB frames; holes are frame-aligned.""",r"""Contiguous feasibility::6+4+4=14\ \text{KiB},\quad \max(6,4,4)=6\ \text{KiB}::Total free memory exceeds the request, but its largest run is only 6 KiB, so ordinary contiguous allocation fails.~Available whole frames::6/2+4/2+4/2=7::The aligned holes provide seven complete frames despite being in separate physical runs.~Paged requirement::\lceil11/2\rceil=6\ \text{frames}::Six of the seven usable frames can hold the process in different physical runs. They allocate 12 KiB for 11 KiB of payload, wasting 1 KiB inside the last page.""", """Contiguous allocation fails from external fragmentation; paged allocation succeeds with six frames and 1 KiB of final-page internal waste.""","""Allocated capacity is 6×2=12 KiB, comprising 11 KiB of payload and 1 KiB of internal waste. The other free frame's 2 KiB remain free memory, not waste inside this process.""")

T('7-virtual-files','demand-page-fault','Virtual memory','A page fault can require writeback, loading and restart',r'''
Virtual memory distinguishes a valid virtual page from a page currently resident in physical memory. Accessing a valid but nonresident page raises a page fault; an invalid address instead requires an error response under the process's mapping rules. A page fault is therefore not simply another name for a TLB miss. A TLB miss may be resolved entirely by reading a page table containing a resident mapping.
Demand paging brings the missing page into a frame when it is needed. If no free frame exists, the replacement policy chooses a victim. A dirty victim must preserve modified contents through writeback when the stated backing-store model requires it; a clean victim can normally be discarded. The OS then loads the requested page, updates mapping and translation state, and restarts the faulting instruction. Count these service components once and distinguish them from the ordinary completed access. The replacement choice, dirty state and cache assumptions can change the cost substantially.
''',"""A valid page P is nonresident. All frames are occupied and the chosen victim Q is dirty. Fault handling costs 0.2 ms, Q writeback 4 ms, P loading 6 ms and restarted access 0.0001 ms; assume these are sequential nonoverlapping costs.""",r"""Validate the fault::::P belongs to the process's valid mapping, so loading is appropriate rather than rejecting an invalid address.~Preserve and replace::::Write back dirty Q for 4 ms, then read P for 6 ms. The same frame is reused; Q's resident mapping must be invalidated.~Finish and restart::T=0.2+4+6+0.0001=10.2001\ \text{ms}::Install the new mapping and complete the restarted instruction, counting the stated overhead once.""", """This access takes 10.2001 ms under the supplied sequential cost model.""","""With a clean victim the writeback disappears, yielding 6.2001 ms. A resident page after a TLB miss would require neither the 4 ms write nor the 6 ms page load.""")

T('7-files','path-metadata-lookup','File systems','A pathname locates metadata before file data',r'''
A file system connects names to file objects and file objects to data blocks. A directory stores mappings from a component name to an identifier such as an inode number. The inode stores the object's type, size, permissions and allocation information. Looking up a pathname therefore follows directory components before the requested byte can be mapped to a physical data block.
Directory reads, inode reads and payload reads are different accesses. A directory entry naming inode 12 is not itself file data and does not imply that the file lives in disk block 12. Cached metadata can remove some disk operations, so an I/O count must state precisely which objects are resident and how they are stored. For a path with multiple components, use the current directory's contents to find the next object, load the required object metadata, and continue. Finally divide the requested byte offset by the block size, preserve the remainder, and consult the allocation map.
''',"""Read byte 200 of /prep/os. Cached root directory identifies prep as inode 7; cached inode 7 points to uncached directory block 50. Block 50 maps os to inode 12, stored in one uncached metadata block; inode 12 maps file logical block 0 to uncached data block 80. Blocks are 1024 bytes.""",r"""Resolve the directory::::Read directory block 50 to discover that os names inode 12. Cached root and inode 7 cost no additional disk reads.~Obtain the file map::::Read the one metadata block containing inode 12; its first data pointer is physical block 80.~Read the payload::200=0(1024)+200::The requested byte is in logical block 0 at offset 200, so read block 80. This is the third disk read.""", """Three reads: prep directory block, os inode metadata block, and os data block.""","""The identifier 12 is used to locate metadata, while 80 is the payload block. If inode 12 were cached, the count would fall to two, demonstrating the cache assumption explicitly.""")

T('7-files','contiguous-byte-lookup','Contiguous allocation','Contiguous allocation maps an offset with arithmetic',r'''
Contiguous allocation gives a file a consecutive run of physical blocks. Metadata needs a starting block and length, so logical block i maps to start+i after checking that i lies within the allocated file. A byte offset additionally splits into a logical block number and a within-block remainder. The remainder remains unchanged when moving to the physical address.
This simple mapping supports direct random access without traversing earlier payload blocks or reading a separate pointer index. Its simplicity does not ensure easy growth. If the physical block immediately after the run is occupied, extending the run can require relocation or another representation even when free blocks exist elsewhere. External fragmentation also makes a large new contiguous request harder to satisfy. State block numbering, block size, file length and cache conditions before counting accesses. An allocation formula cannot justify reading beyond the logical end, and allocated capacity is not necessarily identical to the file's current byte length.
''',"""A six-block file starts at physical block 40, with 1024-byte blocks and cached metadata. Locate byte offset 2700. Physical block 46 is occupied; can the same run expand to seven blocks?""",r"""Split the file offset::2700=2(1024)+652::The byte is in logical block 2 and falls within the six allocated blocks.~Map the physical address::PA=(40+2)(1024)+652=43660::Read physical block 42, using one data-block read under the cache assumption.~Check extension::::The existing run is blocks 40 through 45. Its required next block 46 is occupied, so it cannot simply append a seventh block at this location.""", """Byte offset 2700 maps to physical byte address 43,660 in block 42; immediate contiguous extension fails.""","""43,660 divided by 1024 gives block 42 with remainder 652. Free blocks elsewhere do not make block 46 free; relocating the entire run would be a separate operation.""")

T('7-files','indexed-byte-lookup','Indexed allocation','Indexed allocation needs the right pointer and metadata I/O',r'''
Indexed allocation stores a file's data-block addresses in an index block rather than inside a chain of preceding data blocks. Logical block i selects index entry i, whose value names the physical payload block. The indexed entries need not be consecutive addresses. For a byte request, divide the byte offset by the data-block size and use the remainder inside the selected physical block.
An index is metadata that consumes storage and can require an extra disk read. If the file record naming the index is cached but the index itself is not, a simple one-level random read needs the index block and then the data block. If the index is already cached, only the data read remains. More index levels add their own potential reads. Calculate pointer capacity from index-block size and pointer width after any reserved space, and keep payload bytes separate from metadata bytes. Direct random access means avoiding earlier payload traversal, not automatically avoiding all metadata I/O.
''',"""Cached file metadata names index block 20. Its first six entries are [90,14,78,6,45,82]. Both index and data blocks are uncached; blocks are 1024 bytes and pointers 4 bytes. Locate byte offset 4500 of this six-block file.""",r"""Split the offset::4500=4(1024)+404::Logical block 4 is valid for a six-block file, using zero-based block numbering.~Read the index::::Read block 20 and select entry 4, whose value 45 names the physical data block. Do not interpret 20 or 4 as the payload address.~Read and address data::PA=45(1024)+404=46484::Read physical block 45 and use byte 404. The total is two block reads; the index can hold 1024/4=256 entries.""", """Physical block 45, byte offset 404, physical byte address 46,484; two reads, or one if the index is already cached.""","""Six payload blocks plus one index block occupy seven blocks under this layout. The index's 1024 bytes are metadata and do not increase the six-block file's payload capacity.""")

T('8-er-relations','relation-set-semantics','Relational model','A mathematical relation has set semantics',r'''
The relational model describes a relation as a set of tuples over named attributes and their domains. Attribute names identify values, while tuple order has no mathematical meaning. Because a set has no duplicate members, writing the same entire tuple twice does not create two distinct tuples in the relation. Different tuples can still have the same value for an individual attribute when another attribute differs.
SQL implementations commonly use bag semantics for query results unless duplicate elimination is requested. This difference matters when counting results after dropping attributes. In mathematical relational projection, tuples that become identical are collapsed; SQL SELECT without DISTINCT normally retains their multiplicity. Distinguish an entire-tuple duplicate from a repeated attribute value, and distinguish a relation's set definition from any additional declared key constraint. To work an example, enumerate the full tuples, perform the requested attribute operation, then apply the semantics of that operation before counting the result.
''',"""Consider R(A,B) with tuples (1,x),(2,x),(3,y). Compare relational projection onto B with SQL SELECT B FROM R and SELECT DISTINCT B FROM R.""",r"""Inspect distinct full tuples::::The three full tuples differ on A, so R contains three tuples even though x appears twice as a B value.~Project under the model::\pi_B(R)=\{(x),(y)\}::Dropping A produces repeated (x); set semantics retain it only once.~Compare SQL bags::::SELECT B returns three rows x,x,y. SELECT DISTINCT B removes the repeated output and returns two rows x,y.""", """Relational projection has two tuples; ordinary SQL SELECT has three rows; SELECT DISTINCT has two.""","""COUNT(*) on the ordinary SQL result is 3 while COUNT(DISTINCT B) is 2. Repeated x does not by itself violate relation semantics because its original full tuples differ.""")

T('8-er-relations','foreign-key-actions','Integrity constraints','Foreign keys reject missing parents and govern deletion',r'''
Integrity constraints define legal database states. Domain and check constraints restrict values; primary-key constraints require unique, nonnull identification; foreign keys preserve references to existing parent keys under their declared null and matching rules. A foreign key normally permits many child rows to reference one parent, so it does not establish uniqueness in the child table.
For a nonnull foreign-key value, compare the complete referenced key against parent rows. A missing parent makes the insert or update invalid even when the child has a new unique primary key. Deleting a referenced parent requires the declared referential action. RESTRICT prevents the deletion while references remain; CASCADE deletes matching child rows; SET NULL replaces child references and therefore requires compatible nullability. These are alternative schema choices, not actions that all occur together. Check entity integrity and referential integrity separately, and do not confuse automatic reference enforcement with arbitrary business rules such as grades being within a range.
''',"""Student(id PRIMARY KEY) contains 10 and 20. Enrollment(student_id NOT NULL REFERENCES Student(id), course) contains (10,OS). Test inserting (30,DB), then consider deleting Student 10 under alternative ON DELETE RESTRICT and CASCADE schemas.""",r"""Check the new reference::::30 matches no Student primary key. NOT NULL is satisfied but referential integrity is not, so reject the Enrollment insert.~Apply RESTRICT::::Student 10 is still referenced by (10,OS). The parent deletion is rejected, preserving both original rows.~Apply CASCADE independently::::In the alternative CASCADE schema, delete Student 10 and its referencing Enrollment row together. Student 20 remains; the rejected (30,DB) row never existed.""", """The insert fails. RESTRICT blocks deletion of Student 10; CASCADE removes it and its enrollment.""","""After each accepted operation every remaining nonnull student_id names an existing Student. SET NULL would conflict with this declared NOT NULL column unless the schema were changed.""")

T('8-queries','group-filter-trace','Grouping','WHERE filters rows before HAVING filters groups',r'''
Grouping partitions query rows by the values of the GROUP BY attributes. Aggregates then summarize each partition, and HAVING decides which complete groups survive. WHERE instead filters individual input rows before grouping, so moving a condition between these clauses can change both group membership and aggregate values. This is the logical evaluation model; a database optimizer may choose a different physical execution plan while preserving the result.
COUNT(*) counts surviving rows, COUNT(column) counts their nonnull values, and AVG ignores null operands under ordinary SQL behavior. Group keys can remain even when some rows are removed, provided at least one row survives for that group. To evaluate a grouped query, first enumerate rows that satisfy WHERE, form groups from those rows, compute each aggregate, apply HAVING, and produce selected values. An aggregate test such as COUNT(*)>=2 cannot ordinarily be used as a row predicate in WHERE. Join multiplication must be included before this procedure if the input came from a join.
''',"""Scores(dept,score) has (A,90),(A,70),(A,NULL),(B,85),(B,95),(C,60). Evaluate SELECT dept,COUNT(*),AVG(score) FROM Scores WHERE score>=70 GROUP BY dept HAVING COUNT(*)>=2.""",r"""Filter individual rows::::A's NULL comparison is UNKNOWN and C's 60 fails. Surviving rows are A:90,70 and B:85,95.~Form and summarize groups::A:(2,80),\quad B:(2,90)::COUNT(*) and AVG are calculated from each department's surviving rows.~Filter completed groups::::Both counts equal 2, so both groups pass HAVING. SELECT emits (A,2,80) and (B,2,90), with no guaranteed output order.""", """Two result rows: A with count 2 and average 80; B with count 2 and average 90.""","""The input has six rows but WHERE leaves four. Without WHERE, A would count three rows while its average still ignores NULL; the count difference demonstrates the stage ordering.""")

T('8-normalization','fd-tuple-counterexample','Functional dependencies','A functional dependency constrains pairs of tuples',r'''
A functional dependency X→Y requires any two legal tuples agreeing on every attribute of X to agree on every attribute of Y. It does not require X values to be unique unless X determines the complete schema. To refute the dependency in a specific instance, find just one agreeing-left-side, disagreeing-right-side pair. Tuples with different X values impose no restriction on one another through that dependency.
A sample instance can violate a proposed dependency, but passing a sample test does not establish that the schema guarantees it in every future legal instance. The given dependency set is the source of such guarantees. Determinants can be composite: testing AB→C requires agreement on both A and B, rather than A alone. Distinguish the tuple-level test from attribute closure, which derives what a supplied set of schema dependencies implies. Naming the determinant and searching within its equal-value groups makes a violation concrete instead of relying on accidental uniqueness or visually appealing arrows.
''',"""R(A,B,C) has (1,x,7),(1,x,8),(1,y,7),(2,z,9). Test A→B and AB→C on this instance.""",r"""Test A determinant::::Tuples (1,x,7) and (1,y,7) agree on A=1 but disagree on B. They refute A→B.~Test joint determinant::::Tuples (1,x,7) and (1,x,8) agree on both A and B but disagree on C. They refute AB→C.~Interpret the evidence::::Both violations are pairwise witnesses. Rows with A=2 do not repair either violation; adding more rows cannot make these existing disagreements disappear.""", """This instance violates both A→B and AB→C.""","""Deleting one offending row can repair a particular witness, but all remaining equal-determinant pairs must still be checked. A legal-state guarantee cannot be inferred from one repaired sample alone.""")

T('8-normalization','2nf-composite-key','2NF','2NF removes partial dependencies on a composite key',r'''
Second normal form requires first normal form and prohibits a nonprime attribute from depending on a proper subset of a candidate key. A prime attribute belongs to at least one candidate key. Find all candidate keys before applying the rule: selecting a convenient primary key does not remove other candidate keys from the definition.
A partial dependency often appears when a relationship table stores a fact about only one of its participants. For example, a course enrollment may be identified by student and course while a student name depends only on the student. Repeating the name per enrollment creates update anomalies. To analyze a schema, compute candidate-key closures, determine which attributes are nonprime, and examine dependencies from proper subsets of each composite key. A single-attribute key has no nonempty proper subset, but that shortcut cannot be used when another composite candidate key exists. Separating the dependent fact can remove repetition, with losslessness and dependency enforcement still requiring their own checks.
''',"""Enrollment(S,C,N,G) has SC→G and S→N, with no other nontrivial dependencies and atomic values. Find its key, show the 2NF violation, and split the repeated student-name fact.""",r"""Find the candidate key::(SC)^+=\{S,C,N,G\}::S and C never appear on a dependency's right side, so every key must contain both. SC determines all attributes and is minimal.~Identify the partial dependency::S\subset SC,\quad S\to N::N is nonprime and depends on a proper subset of the sole candidate key. The original relation fails 2NF.~Separate the fact::::Use Student(S,N) and EnrollmentGrade(S,C,G). Their intersection S determines the whole Student component, proving this binary split is lossless.""", """SC is the sole candidate key; S→N violates 2NF. The proposed split removes that partial dependency and is lossless.""","""The retained dependencies S→N and SC→G can each be enforced locally, so this example also preserves its given dependencies. Two courses for one student no longer require duplicate name facts.""")

T('8-normalization','3nf-prime-exception','3NF','3NF can allow a nonkey determinant when the result is prime',r'''
Third normal form tests each nontrivial dependency X→A using two alternatives: X must be a superkey, or A must be prime. Prime means part of some candidate key, not simply part of the selected primary key. BCNF permits only the first alternative, so a schema can satisfy 3NF while failing BCNF.
To use the exception correctly, first derive every candidate key from the supplied dependencies. Then calculate the determinant's closure rather than assuming every left side is a key. If a non-superkey determinant has a prime right-side attribute, that dependency is permitted by 3NF. If the right side is nonprime, it violates 3NF. Dependencies with multiple right-side attributes can be separated for the test. The prime exception is a precise rule; slogans about removing all transitive dependencies can misclassify examples. A proof for a supplied dependency cover must also respect what it implies, and showing that every attribute is prime immediately settles the right-side alternative for any implied dependency here.
''',"""R(A,B,C) has AB→C and C→B, with no other given dependencies. Determine its candidate keys and whether it is in 3NF and BCNF.""",r"""Compute candidate keys::(AB)^+=ABC,\quad (AC)^+=ABC::A must occur in every key because nothing derives it. A alone does not suffice; combining A with B or C does. Thus AB and AC are the minimal keys.~Apply 3NF::::A, B and C are all prime because each belongs to AB or AC. Therefore every nontrivial dependency has a prime right-side attribute, satisfying 3NF.~Apply BCNF::C^+=\{B,C\}\ne ABC::C→B is nontrivial but C is not a superkey, so it violates BCNF despite being allowed by 3NF.""", """Candidate keys AB and AC; the relation is in 3NF and fails BCNF.""","""C cannot determine A, so calling C a key would be incorrect. B is prime through candidate key AB even if AC were chosen as the primary key.""")

T('8-normalization','bcnf-loss-preservation','BCNF','A BCNF split can be lossless without preserving a dependency',r'''
Boyce–Codd normal form requires the determinant of every nontrivial functional dependency to be a superkey. Unlike 3NF, it has no exception for a prime right-side attribute. A violating dependency X→Y suggests a decomposition that separates the attributes governed by X, but the properties of the resulting relations must be proved rather than assumed.
For a binary decomposition, a common sufficient and necessary FD criterion for losslessness is that the shared attributes determine one complete component under the original dependency set. Dependency preservation is a different question: take the dependencies enforceable within individual components and test whether their union still implies every original dependency. A lossless BCNF decomposition may require a join to enforce a former cross-component constraint. Find local candidate keys and local violations to establish the normal form of each component, then use closures to examine preservation. Avoid treating a correct recombination property as proof that all business constraints remain locally enforceable.
''',"""R(A,B,C) has AB→C and C→B. Decompose the BCNF violation C→B into CB and AC. Determine normal form, losslessness and preservation of AB→C.""",r"""Check components::::In CB, C determines both attributes and is a key. AC has no projected nontrivial dependency, so neither component has a nonkey nontrivial determinant; both satisfy BCNF.~Prove losslessness::CB\cap AC=\{C\},\quad C\to CB::The common C determines the whole first component, satisfying the binary lossless criterion.~Test preservation::(AB)^+_{\{C\to B\}}=\{A,B\}::The only nontrivial locally enforceable dependency is C→B. It cannot derive C from AB, so AB→C is not preserved.""", """CB and AC are BCNF and form a lossless split, but the original dependency AB→C is not preserved.""","""AC can contain (a,c1),(a,c2) and CB can contain (c1,b),(c2,b), satisfying local constraints. Their join contains the same AB with two C values, a concrete preservation failure.""")

T('8-storage-index','file-organization-cost','File organization','Heap and sorted files have different search costs',r'''
File organization determines how records occupy data pages before any optional index is considered. A heap file has no search-key ordering, so an equality query without an index may scan pages until it finds the record or proves absence. A sorted file maintains key order, permitting block-level binary search when the layout and access assumptions support it. Maintaining that ordering can make inserts and updates more expensive.
An ordered file also permits continuing sequentially from the first qualifying position for a range, while a heap must examine potentially unrelated pages. These access differences depend on the actual operation: reading most records may favor a sequential scan in either organization. Count page accesses rather than abstract record comparisons, and state whether an index, cache or block directory is available. Sorted organization is not the same as having an unclustered secondary index. To make a numeric comparison, define an explicit searchable page representation, trace its probes, and contrast the unordered search's necessary inspection under the given guarantee.
''',"""An uncached file occupies 16 pages. Each sorted page holds one distinct key, pages 1…16 containing keys 1…16; there is no index. Search for absent key 17. Compare an unordered heap with block binary search using floor((low+high)/2).""",r"""Heap absence proof::::An unordered heap gives no ordering reason to skip any page, so proving key 17 absent requires reading all 16 pages.~Trace sorted probes::::Start low=1, high=16. Read pages 8,12,14,15,16; each key is below 17, so move low above that midpoint.~Terminate the ordered search::low=17>high=16::The sorted search has exhausted its search interval after five reads and proves absence without inspecting the other pages.""", """Heap: 16 reads. The stated sorted binary-search trace: 5 reads.""","""Probe sequence 8→12→14→15→16 covers every successive candidate interval for key 17. The comparison assumes no cached pages or index, so index traversal is not silently omitted.""")

T('8-storage-index','btree-internal-match','B trees','A B-tree search can finish at an internal record',r'''
A B-tree is a balanced multiway search structure whose nodes hold ordered keys and child pointers. In the ordinary B-tree representation used here, record references can occur alongside keys in internal nodes as well as leaves. Compare the target against a node's keys: an equality can finish the search there; otherwise select the child interval that could contain the key and continue.
All leaves have the same depth, and minimum occupancy rules prevent long sparse chains except for the stated root exception. The term order is ambiguous across texts, so specify whether it means maximum children or keys. With at most m children an internal node normally has at most m−1 keys, while the minimum is determined by the supplied convention. Count pages actually visited, not the maximum tree height for every lookup. A B+ tree uses internal keys as routing separators and places record entries at leaves, so equal-looking internal keys do not have the same stopping rule in that different representation.
''',"""A B-tree stores a record reference with every key. Its uncached root contains keys 20 and 50 with three child pointers. Root children are leaves: [5,10], [30,40], [60,70]. Count page reads for independent uncached searches for keys 50 and 40.""",r"""Search for 50::::Read the root. Its stored key 50 has the requested record reference, so the index search finishes immediately after one page read.~Search for 40::::In a fresh uncached search, read the root. Since 20<40<50, follow the middle child.~Complete at the leaf::::Read the [30,40] leaf and find key 40 there. This search visits two index pages, excluding any separately stored payload page.""", """Key 50 needs one index-page read; key 40 needs two under the stated B-tree representation.""","""Both searches respect the root's child intervals. Adding one payload read would be a different cost question; copying the B+ stopping rule would incorrectly force the 50 lookup down to a leaf.""")

T('8-storage-index','bplus-linked-range','B+ trees','A B+ range scan descends once and follows leaf links',r'''
A B+ tree stores record entries in its leaves, with internal separator keys guiding searches to the correct leaf. Leaves are normally linked in sorted order. A range query can therefore descend to the first possible match once and then follow successive leaf links until the upper bound is exceeded, rather than searching from the root separately for every result.
Interpret separator equality using the tree's routing convention. Internal keys are not extra result records, even when they repeat keys found in leaves. Page costs include every uncached internal and leaf page visited; separately stored records can add data-page reads, depending on whether leaf entries contain payload or pointers and whether the data are clustered. The range scan may need the next leaf to discover that no later entries qualify. State exactly which pages and bounds are given before counting. Balance guarantees a common leaf depth, but it does not mean every equality or range operation reads the same number of pages.
''',"""An uncached one-level B+ root has separators 30 and 50: keys <30 use L1, 30…49 use L2, >=50 use L3. Linked leaves contain L1=[10,20], L2=[30,40], L3=[50,60]. Scan inclusive range [20,50]; leaves contain the complete records.""",r"""Find the first leaf::::Read the root and route lower bound 20 to L1. Read L1 and emit 20 while skipping 10.~Follow the leaf chain::::Read linked L2 and emit 30 and 40; no second root traversal is necessary.~Finish the bounded scan::::Read L3, emit 50 and stop at 60 because it exceeds the upper bound. Total reads are one root plus three leaves.""", """Results 20,30,40,50; four page reads, with no additional payload reads in this stated layout.""","""Separator values 30 and 50 are emitted only from leaves, not again from the root. Four qualifying records occupy three leaves, so counting records alone would not give the I/O cost.""")

T('8-transactions','lock-compatibility-wait','Concurrency control','Lock compatibility can block transactions that obey 2PL',r'''
Concurrency control decides which simultaneous operations may proceed while maintaining the database's required isolation guarantees. Shared locks allow compatible readers of the same item; an exclusive lock conflicts with both shared and exclusive locks held by another transaction. A requested lock is not an acquired lock, so an operation that needs it must wait until permission is actually granted.
Two-phase locking imposes acquisition and release phases, but it does not remove all waiting or deadlocks. Transactions can each hold a lock and request another in opposite order while obeying their growing phase. A wait-for graph uses an edge from a waiting transaction to the transaction whose conflicting held lock blocks it. Under this exclusive single-owner lock model, a directed cycle identifies a deadlock. Resolve the blocked execution using the stated detector, victim abort or prevention policy; do not pretend that the conflicting operations have already executed. Serializability guarantees and progress guarantees answer different questions.
''',"""T1 holds exclusive X; T2 holds exclusive Y. Before releasing anything, T1 requests exclusive Y and T2 requests exclusive X. Both use basic 2PL. Explain the outcome.""",r"""Check first request::::T1 cannot acquire Y because T2's exclusive lock conflicts. Add wait edge T1→T2; T1 keeps its existing X lock while waiting.~Check second request::::T2 cannot acquire X because T1 holds it. Add T2→T1, completing a cycle.~Distinguish protocol from progress::::Neither has released a lock, so both remain in the allowed growing phase of 2PL. Nevertheless neither can reach its release without satisfying a blocked request.""", """Both follow the stated 2PL phase rule and become deadlocked; a resolution or prevention mechanism is required.""","""Abort and roll back one chosen victim before releasing its locks, or require a universal X-before-Y order from the start. Basic 2PL's serializability guarantee does not imply freedom from this cycle.""")

T('8-transactions','acid-transfer-guarantees','ACID','Four ACID guarantees apply to different parts of a transfer',r'''
ACID separates four transaction guarantees. Atomicity treats a transaction's operations as one all-or-nothing unit. Consistency requires a successful transaction and constraint enforcement to preserve the database's declared invariants. Isolation governs what concurrent transactions may observe, with serializable isolation presenting an effect equivalent to a permitted serial order. Durability preserves committed effects after failures within the system's stated guarantee.
The properties are related but cannot replace one another. Recovery can undo a partial debit without proving the transfer amount was valid. An application bug can violate a business invariant even when its writes are atomic and durable. Isolation levels weaker than serializability may permit particular anomalies, so an observation rule must name the guarantee being assumed. To classify a scenario, locate whether it concerns an incomplete operation, an invariant, concurrent visibility or a committed result after failure. A transaction's commit marks a boundary: a failure before it calls for atomic recovery, while losing its effects after a successful acknowledged commit challenges durability.
''',"""Initially A=500 and B=300. A transaction transfers 100 from A to B under serializable isolation. Its debit occurs before its credit; another transaction asks for A+B. Consider both a crash before commit and a crash after acknowledged commit.""",r"""Check the invariant::A+B=800,\quad (A,B)_{after}=(400,400)::A completed valid transfer preserves total funds, illustrating consistency of the transaction logic.~Protect partial execution::::Before commit, the debit and credit form one atomic unit. A failed transfer must be undone; serializable observers must obtain a total equivalent to a serial state, rather than treating the intermediate 700 as a completed transfer.~Protect the committed state::::After a successfully acknowledged commit, recovery retains balances 400 and 400 under the durability guarantee. It does not revert a completed transfer simply because a later crash occurs.""", """Atomicity handles incomplete effects; consistency preserves 800; serializable isolation protects concurrent observations; durability retains the committed 400/400 state.""","""Both valid serial orders for the total-reading transaction yield 800. A post-commit recovery returning 500/300 would preserve the sum but still fail durability, demonstrating that the guarantees differ.""")
